## The core problem with the current structure

Tracker / Overlap / Analytics as three flat tabs treats this like a data dashboard. But a student opens an app like this for one of three reasons: *"what should I study right now,"* *"let me log what I just did,"* or *"how am I doing overall."* Nothing in the current structure answers the first question — you land straight in a raw syllabus tree and have to figure out where you left off every single time. That's the biggest UX gap, bigger than colors or chart count.

So I'd restructure around **intent, not data type**.

## New navigation: 4 destinations, not 3

**1. Today (new — this becomes the home screen)**
The first thing you see. Not a syllabus dump.
- "Continue where you left off" — last subject/topic touched
- A short suggested list: topics flagged "revise," sorted by how long they've been sitting untouched (this is the single most useful thing analytics currently buries)
- Today's streak + a simple daily target ("study 5 topics") — optional, dismissible, not preachy
- One-tap access to search

**2. Syllabus** (the current Tracker, redesigned)
- Search bar front and center — this doesn't exist right now and it should. A student mid-scroll trying to find "Rajasthan GK → Fairs & Festivals" shouldn't have to hunt through nested cards.
- Denser subject cards — smaller arc rings, more per row, so the whole syllabus fits in fewer scrolls
- Topic rows: single compact line, swipe-to-complete / swipe-to-flag-for-revision on mobile, hover-reveal actions on desktop — feels native instead of clicky
- Overlap folded in here as a **filter toggle** ("Show shared topics") instead of a separate tab — it's the same data, viewed differently, doesn't need its own destination

**3. Revise**
Pulled out of analytics into its own real view — every topic across every exam currently flagged "revision," sorted by staleness. This is what most tracking apps get wrong: revision flags go in and never come back out anywhere useful. This view is the payoff.

**4. Insights** (trimmed analytics — 5 sections max, not 10)
- Weekly heatmap + streak (merged)
- Per-exam progress
- Subject breakdown
- Pace / estimated completion date
- That's it. Everything else was redundant restating of the same three numbers.

