## Study — syllabus view, rebuilt for speed

- **Search-first.** A real search bar at the top, not buried. Type "fairs" → jump straight to the matching topic under whatever subject, syllabus tree auto-expands to it. This alone removes 80% of scroll-hunting.
- **Command palette (Cmd/Ctrl+K on desktop, search icon on mobile)** — jump to any subject/topic, switch exams, or trigger actions like "export data" from one input. Feels fast, feels modern, costs nothing to build in vanilla JS.
- **Collapsed-by-default subject cards** with a compact progress ring — expand on tap. Currently (per the audit) everything seems to render open and heavy; collapsing by default means the whole syllabus fits above the fold in overview form.
- **Topic rows as single compact lines** — icon-based status (not walls of buttons): a tap cycles not-started → done, a long-press or swipe opens revision/bookmark/notes options. On desktop, hover reveals the same actions inline. This removes visual clutter from the biggest, most-used view in the app.
- **Overlap becomes a filter chip**, not a separate tab: "Shared with [other exam]" toggle at the top of Study. It's the same syllabus data, just filtered — treating it as a whole separate view (as it is now) fragments a workflow that's actually one continuous task: going through your syllabus.
- **Bulk actions** — long-press a subject header → "mark all topics I've covered up to here" for students migrating from a paper tracker. This is a real onboarding accelerator that doesn't exist today.

